\f1\fs22 It was hard to say who was the more furious: the CEO; the Director of Moral Observance; the Director of Freedom; the Commissioner of Elvi Rights; the Refrigeration Ombudsman; or the President of the Humpty Doo Tank Battle Reenactment Club. Fortunately, the tank-enthusiast crocodile-rights-wrestler lacked the security clearance to come within three local governments around Parliament Market.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In a secure conference room designed for neutral corporate chic, an expensively timbered oval table for twenty consumed most of the space. Ambivalently bright lighting rendered the edge of every object in the room sharp as paper folds without stressing the eyes. Expansive artworks\'97abstract contortions of geometry and colour that stimulated neither heart nor intellect\'97hung on reinforced greenish-beige walls. They were insured for sums that could buy basketball teams, mining rights dealers, or the used underwear of famous actors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Attendees sat in ergonomic status-affirming leather chairs and drummed the table with manicured fingernails while a media staffer played a package depicting the attack on Truth\'97PAPER\'92s facility previously considered among the most secure in the land.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tanks,\'92 the Director of Defence said as if swearing. \'91Of all the scenarios\'97attack by air, cyber attack, chemical, biological, espionage, distraction by circus troupe\'97there were none which considered a tank attack.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Why not?\'92 CEO Dunhilton asked, peppering his words with intemperate bile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ulyssia is an island continent. The logistics of putting tanks on our soil and rolling them all the way to a secure location is insurmountable. Tanks are difficult to transport. To be frank, we considered tank attack no more plausible than if a fleet of submarines surfaced in the Western Desert and came knocking at our front door.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chuckles rippled through the room. Only failed African states had submarines any more, and they only ever deployed them for acts of piracy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And yet the tanks rolled,\'92 the Director of Moral Observance observed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They were 
\f2\i our
\f1\i0  tanks,\'92 Gerry Hardman pointed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Defence bristled, already offended that the Commissioner of Police\'97a mere civilian agency\'97was part of what was essentially a council of war. \'91That is not true.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They were ordered by Defence and manufactured here,\'92 Hardman persisted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But never delivered,\'92 Defence defended.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room fell into Antarctic silence. No one was prepared to address the granny in the room. Not that Eloise Shakti was either physically present or a granny (yet), but 
\f2\i granny
\f1\i0  was how all Power thought of her, as if so doing would somehow diminish their terror of her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Since when do we blame an arms dealer for an uprising?\'92 asked the Commissioner of Elvi Rights, whose sole job was to ensure the Elvi had no rights at all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No one,\'92 said Moral Observance. \'91Weapons are good business.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone sighed some anxiety away, and awarded the Moral Observance nods of grateful encouragement.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>CEO Dunhilton addressed the Director of Freedom, whose responsibility was national intelligence, which included Preemptive Reciprocity and Proactive Euthanasia\'97the agency whose motto was 
\f2\i Figere Ante Alios Figere Nos
\f1\i0  (\'91Fix others before they fix us\'92) and was possibly the most lethally paranoid agency, if not in the history of humanity, then certainly since the Mickey Mouse Club. \'91Are we sure the sole objective of the attack was to break out this one traitor, Sukrit Supaporn?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, apart from acquiring Supaporn, the insurgents shot up as many broadcast screens as they could find, quite a few toilet bowls, and a few chandeliers\'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There are chandeliers in Truth?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom equanimously said nothing for a time, then passed a folder down the table for the CEO. The CEO, expecting more, raised his eyebrows as he read the single page\'92s conclusion and scanned up for the reasoning. \'91\'93In the name of the Holy Momma, we\'92ve come to rescue you\'94?\'92 he recited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room chilled, all except Elvi Rights who was electrified with excitement.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No other assets were removed,\'92 Freedom said. \'91The whole offensive withdrew when Supaporn was acquired.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The chill in the room chilled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvi Rights, whose sole Key Performance Insinuator was the containment of the rights she was sworn to uphold, could not contain herself. \'91It is as we feared since the emancipation of the ELVIS units.\'92 She flipped through some documents before her and found a thin pamphlet which she unfolded. \'91I have here a Warranty Card for Grayling Corporation\'92s Entertainment Lifestyle Vocationally Integrated Servant units. It says, in part \'85\'92 she ran her finger down the pamphlet until she found her place and began to read aloud.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i This Warranty is immediately null and void, and the ELVIS shall be immediately reclaimable by GRAYLING on commission of any of the following violations: Feeding the ELVIS after midnight; Feeding the ELVIS bacon fat and coffee grounds; allowing the ELVIS to inhale or choke on rhinestones; Exposing the ELVIS\'97\'92\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just get to the point; we know where you\'92re going,\'92 the Director of Corrections interjected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i \'97to amphetamine, crystal methamphetamine, cocaine, or stimulant patch; Exposing the ELVIS\'97
\f1\i0 ah, here we are. 
\f2\i Exposing the ELVIS to religion or religious memes or icons including but not limited to religious texts, psalms, hymns, dashboard figurines, or gospel music of any kind.\'92 
\f1\i0 Elvi Rights slapped the table as if saying 
\f2\i Case Closed
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It seems this Holy Momma, whoever she is, has voided the warranty of every ELVIS involved in the attack.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room gasped. Any act showing disrespect of a commercial corporation, let alone warranty malfeasance, was considered an act against the state, and was punishable by having all your worldly goods, both white and electrical, repossessed\'97unless of course you were an Airborne with connections.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Jesus Taxing Christ! This Holy Momma could spread religion through the whole Quarter!\'92 said ___. It doesn\'92t really matter who said it, because the meeting was degenerating into chaos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Quarter? Could be the whole country!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No more voices could be heard distinctly above the cacophony, until Agent Jones\'97who had been standing among the entourages of the rest of the directors and commissioners with their backs up against the artworks\'97stepped forward, seized a pencil from someone\'92s hand, snapped it as loud as cracking a neck, and slammed it on the table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Into the renaissance of silence, the CEO said, \'91We must consider the worst possible outcome: the entire population of the Elvi contaminated by religion.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A religion other than Latter Day Capitalism,\'92 Moral Observance distinguished.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed,\'92 said the CEO, and all at table put their hands over their hearts where, in their breast pockets, they kept their Coins of Allegiance. The CEO told Elvi Rights, \'91I move we invoke the emergency provisions clause of the Grayling Entertainment Units Emancipation Contract. Will you oppose?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax, no!\'92 Elvi Rights chuckled. Recovering her professional persona, she added, \'91By which, I mean, given the unprecedented circumstances we find ourselves in, I will refrain from using my powers to oppose.\'92 She smiled with glee, already counting the pyramid of enrichment such acquiescence will bring her come annual bonus time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Very well,\'92 said the CEO, and to the whole table, \'91Options?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Concentration Camps,\'92 stated the Director of Corrections, seeking to upholster his budget.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We can\'92t imprison them all,\'92 Gerry said, matter-of-fact. \'91We don\'92t have the resources.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I meant off-shore, of course,\'92 Corrections negotiated. \'91I have a portfolio of health services orgs who are busting to branch out into prisons\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They certainly have the expertise.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'97and would be willing to exact unconscionably inflated fees, due to the range of consultants and retainers they\'92d need to ensure an acceptable overrun of implementation.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Several players at the table began counting beach homes in their heads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, clearly I can\'92t suggest this,\'92 Elvi Rights said, \'91I want to go on record as opposing \'85 we should wipe them out entirely?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It would mean we could finally begin redeveloping the Ghetto,\'92 said the Director of Housing, \'91and other Elvi enclaves around the country.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Several players at the table began counting high-rise apartments in their heads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We can\'92t go wiping them out before the World Elvis Convention,\'92 complained the Director of Tourism, who couldn\'92t figure out how he\'92d profit by it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How would the international Elvi communities react if we killed off their kind?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If our Elvi have got religion, the rest of the world might quarantine us,\'92 said the Director of Foreign Affairs and Sexual Diplomacy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If we exterminate them that could make them martyrs in the eyes of international Elvi. We may export this religion\'97which ever way we jump.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We have to make it look like an accident, or a tragedy\'97the Hand of Homer, or something,\'92 Gerry said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom exchanged furtive glances with Gerry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you have a proposal?\'92 the CEO challenged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry swivelled a file around and skidded it across the table for the CEO. As it came to rest, Dunhilton saw enough of its title to clamp his hand over it, and order, \'91Everyone out.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Grumbling but compliant, everyone rose to leave, except for Gerry and Freedom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You too,\'92 Dunhilton told his number one security officer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom glared at Gerry, then smirked. He knew Gerry was a clandestine PAPER agent, so he\'92d be able to bring him back into the fold when the time came. Freedom vowed to himself he\'92d make the debrief entertaining.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As they left, it was hard to say who was the more furious, but the unfolding of history would vindicate placing the shortest odds on Special Agent Jones and the Refrigeration Ombudsman.\

